Starship presets and compatibility data
---------------------------------------
Source: https://github.com/starship/starship/tree/v1.24.2
Version: 1.24.2
Upstream files: docs/public/presets/toml/*.toml
Local files: fixtures/import/presets/*.toml, their *.expected.json import
goldens, fixtures/import/author-pastel-powerline.*, the benchmark preset in
bench/zdotdir-starship_user/starship.toml, and the Starship-derived presets in
src/config/presets/*.json.
The Catppuccin preset is expanded into flavor variants; pastel-powerline
adds a second prompt row and status modules. Tildy's importer translates
the upstream TOML into Tildy's JSON configuration model.
License: ISC; complete upstream notice in licenses/starship.txt.
Directory/language/package/environment providers implement compatible
behavior independently, as documented in their source headers.

Color palettes
--------------
src/config/palettes/*.json expresses named upstream palettes in Tildy's
format and adds semantic roles (bg1–bg6, accent, ok, warn, err, etc.).
Source URLs and retrieved license texts are in licenses/sources.json and
licenses/*.txt. Palette authors retain rights to their original material.
The original import commit did not record exact palette revisions; these
license copies were checked against upstream on 2026-11-07. This does not
claim that the original revisions were pinned.

Gruvbox colors: morhetz, https://github.com/morhetz/gruvbox.
The upstream README identifies its license as MIT/X11. Attribution and
license terms are reproduced in licenses/gruvbox.txt.

Tokyo Night: https://github.com/folke/tokyonight.nvim, Apache-2.0.
Only color data is adapted, with new Tildy semantic role mappings; no Vim/Lua
implementation is included. See licenses/tokyo-night.txt.

Powerlevel10k inspired the lean/classic layouts and prompt interaction design.
Its implementation and fonts are not bundled. Nerd Font glyphs refer to fonts
installed by the user; no font software is distributed with Tildy.

Rust dependencies
-----------------
Cargo.lock records exact dependency versions. Release builds collect registry
packages' license texts and metadata with scripts/collect-licenses.py and
include them under licenses/dependencies in each binary archive.
